    "q-45602": {
      "dom_id": "step-q45602",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "The mass, in metric tons, of a meteor is modeled by the function $m(t)=20\\cdot (0.4)^{t},$ where $t$ represents the time, in minutes, since the meteor entered the Earth's atmosphere. In metric tons, how much mass did the meteor lose between the second and third minutes after entering the Earth's atmosphere?",
      "worked_solution": "We need to find the total mass that the meteor lost between $t=2$ and $t=3$ minutes. To calculate this, we find the meteor's mass after $2$ minutes, and we subtract its mass after $3$ minutes. So we need to evaluate\n\n$$\nm(2)-m(3).\n$$\n\nThe mass of the meteor after $t=2$ minutes is equal to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m(2) & =20\\cdot (0.4)^{2} \\\\  & =20\\cdot 0.16 \\\\  & =3.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe mass of the meteor after $t=3$ minutes is equal to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m(3) & =20\\cdot (0.4)^{3} \\\\  & =20\\cdot 0.064 \\\\  & =1.28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass lost between the second and third minute is\n\n$$\nm(2)-m(3)=3.2-1.28=1.92.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the meteor's mass decreased by $1.92$ metric tons between the second and third minutes.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
